WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.634 --> 00:00:02.868
-Our next guest is a very
funny actress, comedian,

00:00:02.868 --> 00:00:04.537
and best-selling author
you know from her work

00:00:04.537 --> 00:00:07.206
in movies like "Obvious Child"
and shows such as

00:00:07.206 --> 00:00:09.342
"Parks and Recreation"
and "The Great North."

00:00:09.342 --> 00:00:12.912
She co-created, co-wrote,
voiced, and produced

00:00:12.912 --> 00:00:14.447
"Marcel the Shell
With Shoes On,"

00:00:14.447 --> 00:00:16.115
which is in select theaters
this Friday.

00:00:16.115 --> 00:00:17.250
Let's take a look.

00:00:17.250 --> 00:00:18.417
-Rolling. Give me some levels.

00:00:18.417 --> 00:00:22.155
-Give -- give you some levels?
Like --?

00:00:22.155 --> 00:00:24.057
-Just, like, talk.
-Like --

00:00:24.057 --> 00:00:26.359
Oh, uh -- Hello.

00:00:26.359 --> 00:00:27.727
My name is Marc-- Mar...

00:00:27.727 --> 00:00:28.861
[ Sighs ]

00:00:28.861 --> 00:00:30.096
It's not the first time
I've done that.

00:00:30.096 --> 00:00:31.864
My name is Marcel,

00:00:31.864 --> 00:00:35.401
and I'm partially shell,
as you can see on my body.

00:00:35.401 --> 00:00:41.374
But I also have shoes and...

00:00:41.374 --> 00:00:42.775
a face.

00:00:42.775 --> 00:00:45.411
-Please welcome back to the
show, our very good friend,

00:00:45.411 --> 00:00:48.281
Jenny Slate, everybody!

00:00:48.281 --> 00:00:51.117
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:51.117 --> 00:00:58.157 line:0%
♪♪

00:00:58.157 --> 00:01:05.231 line:0%
♪♪

00:01:05.231 --> 00:01:09.035
-Jenny, you look wonderful.
-What the heck?

00:01:09.035 --> 00:01:10.636
-I should have kept my
suit on.

00:01:10.636 --> 00:01:12.805
-I know it. I know.

00:01:12.805 --> 00:01:15.141
Or it's just weird that you
also didn't have a --

00:01:15.141 --> 00:01:16.542
-Oh, you should
have brought me one.

00:01:16.542 --> 00:01:19.946
-Yeah, John stole my bit,
so I just left it, you know?

00:01:19.946 --> 00:01:22.048
-You look very --
you look so beautiful.

00:01:22.048 --> 00:01:23.316
So put together.

00:01:23.316 --> 00:01:24.983
But you had quite an ordeal
getting here today.

00:01:24.983 --> 00:01:26.553
-Here's what happened.

00:01:26.553 --> 00:01:29.054
And this is a real, fresh story.
It's really real.

00:01:29.054 --> 00:01:30.590
-This is the today story.

00:01:30.590 --> 00:01:32.558
-This is -- Yeah, this is
really what happened.

00:01:32.558 --> 00:01:36.462
So, like most people who
go on an airplane,

00:01:36.462 --> 00:01:39.298
I fell asleep on the way to
the airport.

00:01:39.298 --> 00:01:41.367
Got on the plane, and
immediately was like --

00:01:41.367 --> 00:01:42.668
[ Snoring ]

00:01:42.668 --> 00:01:44.270
Like, just down, just out.

00:01:44.270 --> 00:01:46.439
And then, like, woke up
just long enough to

00:01:46.439 --> 00:01:49.842
recline a tiny bit more and just
get deeper into that slumber.

00:01:49.842 --> 00:01:51.644
And then woke up when
the plane was like --

00:01:51.644 --> 00:01:53.112
[ Imitates metal creaking ]
You know?

00:01:53.112 --> 00:01:57.049
And then woke up, ripped open
a thing of, like, cashews

00:01:57.049 --> 00:01:58.985
and was like glug, glug, glug.

00:01:58.985 --> 00:02:00.653
Just a class act.

00:02:00.653 --> 00:02:01.987
Class act all the way through.

00:02:01.987 --> 00:02:04.457
And waltzed off the plane,

00:02:04.457 --> 00:02:06.726
and when I got out
of the airport,

00:02:06.726 --> 00:02:11.764
I realized that I had left
this dress on the airplane.

00:02:11.764 --> 00:02:14.400
-Oh, like,
a carry-on garment bag?

00:02:14.400 --> 00:02:16.169
-Yeah, because I was like --

00:02:16.169 --> 00:02:22.408
My stylist, who helped me find
a non-sweatsuit outfit.

00:02:22.408 --> 00:02:23.776
-Yeah.

00:02:23.776 --> 00:02:27.079
-Because I'm a mom,
like, splattered in --

00:02:27.079 --> 00:02:28.314
-Baby -- yeah.

00:02:28.314 --> 00:02:29.482
-Literally
anything you can throw.

00:02:29.482 --> 00:02:31.417
-Baby detritus, yeah.
-Oh, big time, big time.

00:02:31.417 --> 00:02:32.685
That's my look.

00:02:32.685 --> 00:02:34.520
He was like, "Oh, you know
you have to actually,

00:02:34.520 --> 00:02:35.888
like, get dressed up
and do this."

00:02:35.888 --> 00:02:38.925
And so I was like,
"Oh, I'll shove it in my bag."

00:02:38.925 --> 00:02:40.326
And he was like,
"No, no, no, no, no.

00:02:40.326 --> 00:02:41.694
You're not going to
shove it in your bag.

00:02:41.694 --> 00:02:43.129
You're going to hang it up
on the plane."

00:02:43.129 --> 00:02:45.398
And I was like, "Of course.
Of course I'm gonna do that."

00:02:45.398 --> 00:02:46.532
And I did, but then
I left it there.

00:02:46.532 --> 00:02:47.733
[ Laughter ]

00:02:47.733 --> 00:02:50.202
Waltzed right off,
left it on the plane.

00:02:50.202 --> 00:02:52.038
And then like -- and also,

00:02:52.038 --> 00:02:53.239
I was wearing sweat pants
on the plane.

00:02:53.239 --> 00:02:54.273
-Yeah.

00:02:54.273 --> 00:02:56.242
-Got to the lady
at the Jetblue desk.

00:02:56.242 --> 00:02:58.577
I was -- I was crying.
-Yeah.

00:02:58.577 --> 00:03:01.113
-I also took off my mask
so she could see my frown.

00:03:01.113 --> 00:03:04.584
So she would know it was like,
a full thing.

00:03:04.584 --> 00:03:06.719
And yeah, and they had
to find this for me.

00:03:06.719 --> 00:03:08.387
And I just, I really --

00:03:08.387 --> 00:03:11.457
I was on the verge of
sweatsuit, but in fact,

00:03:11.457 --> 00:03:13.058
it's this.

00:03:13.058 --> 00:03:14.227
-Seems like we maybe owe

00:03:14.227 --> 00:03:15.862
a shout out
to the good people at Jetblue.

00:03:15.862 --> 00:03:17.296
-I know, I know.

00:03:17.296 --> 00:03:19.799
I just want to say thank you
to the lady at the Jetblue desk.

00:03:19.799 --> 00:03:21.601
I was like, "I'm going
to give you a shout out.

00:03:21.601 --> 00:03:23.736
I'm going on
a television show tonight.

00:03:23.736 --> 00:03:26.372
It's Seth Meyers."
She said, "I love that show."

00:03:26.372 --> 00:03:28.607
-Oh, wow.
-Okay?

00:03:28.607 --> 00:03:30.042
She said, "I love that show."

00:03:30.042 --> 00:03:31.644
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:03:31.644 --> 00:03:34.613
-So, this is --
I'm always happy to see you.

00:03:34.613 --> 00:03:37.250
This is such a wonderful reason
to have you here.

00:03:37.250 --> 00:03:39.418
"Marcel the Shell"
was a short film,

00:03:39.418 --> 00:03:42.721
a stop-motion film
you made years ago.

00:03:42.721 --> 00:03:44.190
You posted it online,

00:03:44.190 --> 00:03:47.526
and it became -- in my mind,
it's one of the sort of earlier

00:03:47.526 --> 00:03:48.728
YouTube sensations.

00:03:48.728 --> 00:03:50.263
Is that accurate,
my memory of it?

00:03:50.263 --> 00:03:52.298
-That is accurate.
Your memory is accurate.

00:03:52.298 --> 00:03:53.632
Yeah, it is.

00:03:53.632 --> 00:03:54.801
It's sort of a wonder,

00:03:54.801 --> 00:03:56.703
because anyone who knows
me knows I hardly know

00:03:56.703 --> 00:03:57.870
how to turn on my computer.

00:03:57.870 --> 00:03:59.405
-Yeah.

00:03:59.405 --> 00:04:01.407
-Yeah, but Dean Fleischer-Camp,
who I made the short film with,

00:04:01.407 --> 00:04:05.645
posted it on the Internet
after showing it to, like,

00:04:05.645 --> 00:04:07.847
12 people at a comedy show
in Brooklyn.

00:04:07.847 --> 00:04:09.115
And he showed it,

00:04:09.115 --> 00:04:10.983
and one of them was like,
"That's such a nice thing.

00:04:10.983 --> 00:04:12.017
Could I show it --"

00:04:12.017 --> 00:04:13.185
I think,
"Can I show it to my mom?"

00:04:13.185 --> 00:04:14.686
Who, like, had
a broken leg or something.

00:04:14.686 --> 00:04:16.155
And so he put it
on the internet,

00:04:16.155 --> 00:04:17.456
and we just thought it would be

00:04:17.456 --> 00:04:19.591
for this one person, basically,
who wanted to

00:04:19.591 --> 00:04:21.827
send a happy,
little thing to their mom.

00:04:21.827 --> 00:04:25.931
And instead it went much,
much wider than that.

00:04:25.931 --> 00:04:27.099
-Yeah.
-Yeah.

00:04:27.099 --> 00:04:28.333
-You obviously --

00:04:28.333 --> 00:04:30.669
The voice is one of the
more endearing qualities,

00:04:30.669 --> 00:04:32.171
although there are many
endearing qualities

00:04:32.171 --> 00:04:33.706
about Marcel.

00:04:33.706 --> 00:04:36.109
Do you -- you now have
an 18-month-old, Ida.

00:04:36.109 --> 00:04:39.279
Do you read to her
in Marcel's voice?

00:04:39.279 --> 00:04:42.047
-Yeah, I speak to her.
I sing to her.

00:04:42.047 --> 00:04:44.650
She loves --
-She does. That's very good.

00:04:44.650 --> 00:04:45.818
-Getting read to in this voice.
Yeah.

00:04:45.818 --> 00:04:47.286
-That's great. Do you want --

00:04:47.286 --> 00:04:48.988
Could you read to us
from a kids' book

00:04:48.988 --> 00:04:50.022
of what it sounds like?

00:04:50.022 --> 00:04:51.023
-I mean, you wouldn't happen to

00:04:51.023 --> 00:04:52.858
have a kids' book behind this?

00:04:52.858 --> 00:04:53.993
-I do. I wrote one.
-Oh, my God!

00:04:53.993 --> 00:04:55.261
-It's, like,
available in stores.

00:04:55.261 --> 00:04:56.496
It's no big deal.
-Oh, wow.

00:04:56.496 --> 00:04:57.629
-Here.

00:04:57.629 --> 00:04:59.365
-So people could,
like, they could buy this?

00:04:59.365 --> 00:05:00.433
-They could buy it, I guess.

00:05:00.433 --> 00:05:01.834
It's, like, in stores.
It's super weird.

00:05:01.834 --> 00:05:03.803
-Yeah, that's like, so weird.
-There you go.

00:05:03.803 --> 00:05:05.237
You start from the beginning,
so you don't spoil the ending.

00:05:05.237 --> 00:05:08.206
-Got it, got it. Got it.

00:05:08.206 --> 00:05:09.776
"There once was a bear...

00:05:09.776 --> 00:05:11.944
[ Laughter ]

00:05:11.944 --> 00:05:13.779
...who was easily scared."

00:05:13.779 --> 00:05:14.947
Oh, that's really sad.

00:05:14.947 --> 00:05:17.917
-Yeah. It stays sad.
It never gets better.

00:05:17.917 --> 00:05:19.718
[ Laughter ]

00:05:19.718 --> 00:05:21.721
-"Each night
before going to sleep,

00:05:21.721 --> 00:05:23.689
he would tie a bell
to his door that would

00:05:23.689 --> 00:05:26.258
make a noise if anyone
tried to sneak in.

00:05:26.258 --> 00:05:28.161
Because even a bear

00:05:28.161 --> 00:05:31.463
who is easily scared
is a very heavy sleeper."

00:05:31.463 --> 00:05:33.866
That's true. That's true.
-Bravo.

00:05:33.866 --> 00:05:34.900
[ Applause ]

00:05:34.900 --> 00:05:36.835
So I feel like --
-That's really cute.

00:05:36.835 --> 00:05:38.203
-Thank you.
I feel like your daughter is

00:05:38.203 --> 00:05:39.872
going to have
incredibly good hearing,

00:05:39.872 --> 00:05:41.840
because Marcel does not project.

00:05:41.840 --> 00:05:43.508
-He can't project at all.

00:05:43.508 --> 00:05:48.213
That's why I have to ram my
finger into my own ear.

00:05:48.213 --> 00:05:51.984
-Now, you are obviously
one of the voices in this.

00:05:51.984 --> 00:05:55.521
And then you reached out and you
got, for Marcel's grandmother --

00:05:55.521 --> 00:05:57.990
-Yeah.
-A real incredible pull.

00:05:57.990 --> 00:06:01.126
-It's Isabella Rossellini.
-Which is amazing.

00:06:01.126 --> 00:06:03.229
-Can you believe it?
-How did it happen?

00:06:03.229 --> 00:06:05.931
-So we wanted
Marcel's grandmother,

00:06:05.931 --> 00:06:07.099
whose name is Nana Connie,

00:06:07.099 --> 00:06:08.400
which is also
the name of my grandmother,

00:06:08.400 --> 00:06:10.069
whose name is Nana Connie.

00:06:10.069 --> 00:06:11.236
We wanted her

00:06:11.236 --> 00:06:13.906
to have a distinctive voice,
maybe an accent.

00:06:13.906 --> 00:06:15.341
One of my grandmothers
has an accent.

00:06:15.341 --> 00:06:16.609
And so we were like,

00:06:16.609 --> 00:06:19.011
"Well, it's a long shot,
but maybe we'll just ask.

00:06:19.011 --> 00:06:20.046
Whatever."

00:06:20.046 --> 00:06:21.814
And they presented it to her

00:06:21.814 --> 00:06:24.050
and she was like,
"I don't know what this is.

00:06:24.050 --> 00:06:25.417
[ Laughter ]

00:06:25.417 --> 00:06:26.853
Never heard of it."

00:06:26.853 --> 00:06:30.156
And her children were like,
"No, we like Marcel the Shell.

00:06:30.156 --> 00:06:32.224
You should do it.
You should do it."

00:06:32.224 --> 00:06:34.526
And so she just said yes,

00:06:34.526 --> 00:06:37.729
kind of like, just because
her kids said it was worth it.

00:06:37.729 --> 00:06:39.599
But we didn't even have
a script.

00:06:39.599 --> 00:06:42.535
The film is improvised off
of a treatment.

00:06:42.535 --> 00:06:45.605
Like, a long document,
basically.

00:06:45.605 --> 00:06:47.640
And so she showed up
and was like --

00:06:47.640 --> 00:06:49.475
-And showed up
not at a studio.

00:06:49.475 --> 00:06:51.511
-Right, right.
That's the other thing.

00:06:51.511 --> 00:06:53.946
It's not like "Zootopia,"
where you show up

00:06:53.946 --> 00:06:55.014
and they're like, "Hi, this Is

00:06:55.014 --> 00:06:57.116
all the hors d'oeuvrs
in the world."

00:06:57.116 --> 00:06:59.152
-Yeah.
-You know, like, "We're Disney.

00:06:59.152 --> 00:07:02.321
Would you like, you know,
a pheasant..."

00:07:02.321 --> 00:07:05.191
-Hummus.
-"A pheasant hummus?"

00:07:05.191 --> 00:07:08.394
Which they're famous for at
Disney, is the pheasant hummus.

00:07:08.394 --> 00:07:10.696
-You really got
to grind up the pheasant.

00:07:10.696 --> 00:07:13.332
[ Laughter ]

00:07:13.332 --> 00:07:15.400
-I just filled up
on that ground-up pheasant.

00:07:15.400 --> 00:07:19.805
Anyway, she came to our rental,

00:07:19.805 --> 00:07:22.608
where we were recording
with microphones taped

00:07:22.608 --> 00:07:23.743
to the middle of our heads.

00:07:23.743 --> 00:07:26.412
This is what you call
an indie film.

00:07:26.412 --> 00:07:28.814
And we had nothing to offer.

00:07:28.814 --> 00:07:30.349
Like, we had like,
some snacks and stuff,

00:07:30.349 --> 00:07:32.284
but we were like,
"So, Isabella Rossellini,

00:07:32.284 --> 00:07:33.352
what do you like on Postmates?"

00:07:33.352 --> 00:07:34.420
Like it was --

00:07:34.420 --> 00:07:35.521
-Did you ever order

00:07:35.521 --> 00:07:36.989
Postmates for
Isabella Rossellini?

00:07:36.989 --> 00:07:39.858
-Yeah, we got Thai food.
We got Thai food.

00:07:39.858 --> 00:07:41.027
-I think, you know,

00:07:41.027 --> 00:07:42.962
the Hollywood gossip mongers
want to know,

00:07:42.962 --> 00:07:45.498
what does Isabella Rossellini
order from a Thai restaurant?

00:07:45.498 --> 00:07:47.266
-You know,
I can't exactly remember,

00:07:47.266 --> 00:07:50.036
but I'm thinking, like,
"I'll have the summer rolls."

00:07:50.036 --> 00:07:51.437
[ Laughter ]

00:07:51.437 --> 00:07:54.539
You know, like, beautiful,
beautiful, beautiful voice.

00:07:54.539 --> 00:07:57.009
-You know, obviously,
this is a summer movie.

00:07:57.009 --> 00:07:58.444
It makes sense.

00:07:58.444 --> 00:08:01.080
It's a giant, big-budget, as we
established, summer movie.

00:08:01.080 --> 00:08:02.314
-Yeah.

00:08:02.314 --> 00:08:03.448
-And what would you say to
the people who are,

00:08:03.448 --> 00:08:04.483
you know, maybe it's like,

00:08:04.483 --> 00:08:05.584
are we going to go see
"Maverick"?

00:08:05.584 --> 00:08:06.618
Are we going to go see

00:08:06.618 --> 00:08:07.920
"Marcel the Shell
With Shoes on?"

00:08:07.920 --> 00:08:09.422
Like, how do you help them
make that choice?

00:08:09.422 --> 00:08:10.522
-Sure.

00:08:10.522 --> 00:08:12.124
I mean, let me say on camera,

00:08:12.124 --> 00:08:16.629
I don't want to insult
Tom Cruise in any way.

00:08:16.629 --> 00:08:19.565
So I think you should
maybe see both films.

00:08:19.565 --> 00:08:22.034
But I definitely think
you should see ours.

00:08:22.034 --> 00:08:24.370
And -- the other thing is,

00:08:24.370 --> 00:08:26.205
I heard a rumor, truly,

00:08:26.205 --> 00:08:30.242
when I was on the
"Alvin and the Chipmunks" movie.

00:08:30.242 --> 00:08:34.380
That's how all
Hollywood stuff starts.

00:08:34.380 --> 00:08:36.181
"When I was there talking
to an invisible chipmunk."

00:08:36.181 --> 00:08:37.683
No, truly what I remember

00:08:37.683 --> 00:08:39.518
is someone, like, popping
their head out of a plant

00:08:39.518 --> 00:08:42.355
and being like, "Tom Cruise is
watching 'Marcel the Shell.'"

00:08:42.355 --> 00:08:44.289
They told me that he watched it.

00:08:44.289 --> 00:08:45.458
-Wow.
-Yeah.

00:08:45.458 --> 00:08:47.192
-That just, like,
percolated through Hollywood

00:08:47.192 --> 00:08:48.627
till it found you on set?

00:08:48.627 --> 00:08:51.163
-Completely, yeah.
-Wow.

00:08:51.163 --> 00:08:53.532
-Which is crazy, because,
like, on that movie,

00:08:53.532 --> 00:08:55.568
like, I am a human,
but the chipmunks are there,

00:08:55.568 --> 00:08:56.936
and, of course,
they're, like, invisible.

00:08:56.936 --> 00:08:59.237
I hate to break it to you,
but, yeah.

00:08:59.237 --> 00:09:01.574
So there was kind of like,
no one on set also.

00:09:01.574 --> 00:09:02.708
-Yeah.

00:09:02.708 --> 00:09:04.477
-Someone being like,
"ba, ba, ba."

00:09:04.477 --> 00:09:06.512
But I always sort of
held it with me,

00:09:06.512 --> 00:09:07.913
and now that the movie's
coming out,

00:09:07.913 --> 00:09:10.783
I know Tom's probably doing
a lot of press for his film.

00:09:10.783 --> 00:09:12.284
-Yeah, I think he's out there.
Yeah.

00:09:12.284 --> 00:09:15.721
-Yeah, I'm just sort of like,
"Sir, I really enjoy your work.

00:09:15.721 --> 00:09:18.223
If you do like Marcel,
maybe you could just

00:09:18.223 --> 00:09:22.194
share a little bit of
your spotlight with us.

00:09:22.194 --> 00:09:24.296
And please let me know
if you want tickets."

00:09:24.296 --> 00:09:26.131
[ Laughter ]
-That's so nice.

00:09:26.131 --> 00:09:27.499
-Yeah.
-That would be so nice.

00:09:27.499 --> 00:09:29.435
-It would be so nice

00:09:29.435 --> 00:09:32.905
if Tom Cruise came to see
the Marcel movie.

00:09:32.905 --> 00:09:35.274
-You know, in the beginning
of "Maverick,"

00:09:35.274 --> 00:09:36.509
there's a video of him
being like,

00:09:36.509 --> 00:09:37.943
"Thanks so much for
coming out to the movie."

00:09:37.943 --> 00:09:39.077
-Yeah.
-Yeah.

00:09:39.077 --> 00:09:40.178
I feel like if he comes,

00:09:40.178 --> 00:09:41.280
you should record
yourself being like,

00:09:41.280 --> 00:09:43.215
"Thank you, Tom Cruise,
for coming."

00:09:43.215 --> 00:09:44.483
-For sure.

00:09:44.483 --> 00:09:47.586
Like, I should probably just
record that super quick.

00:09:47.586 --> 00:09:50.756
-Yeah, so let's go right there,
and we'll play this.

00:09:50.756 --> 00:09:52.458
If he comes,
this will start the screening.

00:09:52.458 --> 00:09:53.759
-If he comes.

00:09:53.759 --> 00:09:55.061
Hi, Tom.

00:09:55.061 --> 00:09:56.261
[ Laughter ]

00:09:56.261 --> 00:09:58.998
It's Jenny Slate,
the voice, co-creator, writer,

00:09:58.998 --> 00:10:01.000
and co-producer of the film
you're about to see.

00:10:01.000 --> 00:10:02.468
I just want to thank you so much

00:10:02.468 --> 00:10:03.935
for taking the time
out of your busy schedule

00:10:03.935 --> 00:10:05.504
to see our small film.

00:10:05.504 --> 00:10:07.105
Indie film really,

00:10:07.105 --> 00:10:09.508
really appreciates
everyone that watches it.

00:10:09.508 --> 00:10:11.443
And I love you and I've always
thought you were really cute.

00:10:11.443 --> 00:10:13.813
My favorite movie of yours
is "The Client."

00:10:13.813 --> 00:10:15.247
[ Laughter ]

00:10:15.247 --> 00:10:16.615
No, "The Firm"!

00:10:16.615 --> 00:10:20.018
Oh, no, Tom, "The Firm."

00:10:20.018 --> 00:10:22.955
We can edit. We can edit.

00:10:22.955 --> 00:10:24.189
-Is he even in "The Client"?

00:10:24.189 --> 00:10:25.558
-No, it's Sarandon!

00:10:25.558 --> 00:10:27.793
[ Laughter ]

00:10:27.793 --> 00:10:29.495
Tom, I'm so sorry.

00:10:29.495 --> 00:10:30.963
-Oh, my God, this went terribly.

00:10:30.963 --> 00:10:32.732
Jenny Slate, everybody!

00:10:32.732 --> 00:10:34.399
"Marcel the Shell with Shoes On"

00:10:34.399 --> 00:10:36.202
is in select theaters
on June 24th

00:10:36.202 --> 00:10:37.436
and nationwide on July 15th.

